{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_sxidwcpw4qpunsokyfenomrutdhwoymaeji5hsrqrcwatywdcxha","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases complete. The translation is now byte-identical to the C.\n\n## What the program does\n\n`c_src/src/main.c` reads one float with `scanf(\"%f\")` and prints its 4 raw bytes as hex. Because `x` is pre-initialized to `+0.0f` and `scanf`'s return value is **ignored**, a matching failure is observable as `00000000` — and it's distinguishable from a successful `-0.0` conversion (`00000080`) only by the sign bit. That gap is where most of the bugs lived.\n\n## Bugs found and fixed (4)\n\n1. **`-0x` printed `-0.0` instead of `+0.0`.** The Rust treated \"`0x` with no hex digits\" as a successful conversion of the leading `0`. A scratch probe (printing `scanf`'s return value and the next unread char) showed glibc reports a *matching failure*, leaving `x` at `+0.0`. Invisible for `0x`; only the negative spelling exposes it. `scan_hex` now returns `Option<f32>`.\n\n2. **`-0x.` must still convert, as signed zero.** The counterpart that constrains the fix above: glibc accepts a `.` in place of a hex digit and *succeeds* with a signed zero, without even looking at a following `p`. A naive \"no digits ⇒ failure\" fix would have broken this.\n\n3. **Exponent clamped to ±10⁶, which a long significand cancels.** `1` + 1000060 zeros + `e-1000060` is genuinely `1.0`, but Rust printed infinity. A clamp is only sound if the digits can't cancel it. Fixed by raising saturation to 10¹⁸ and renormalizing the decimal form to `0.<digits>e<E>` so the formatted exponent is always tiny while long significands stay exact; the hex path now accumulates in `i128`.\n\n4. **Broken stdout pipe: C dies from `SIGPIPE` (‑13), Rust exited 0.** The Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`. Restored `SIG_DFL`. Verified `/dev/full` still exits 0 in both, so only the signal case changed.\n\n## Notable non-bugs (verified, not \"fixed\")\n\n`strtod` sets a NaN payload from `nan(n-char-seq)`, but glibc's `scanf(\"%f\")` does **not** — it matches bare `nan`, leaves `(` unread, and always yields `0x7fc00000`. Also confirmed: truncated `infi`/`infin` are matching failures, dangling `1e`/`0x1p` convert with exponent 0, and `.`/`e5`/bare `+` fail.\n\n## Verification\n\n`translation/tests/differential.rs` — 34 tests, no `#[ignore]`, spawning both binaries and comparing stdout, stderr, exit code **and terminating signal**. Comparing signals mattered: a signalled process reports `code() == None`, so an exit-code-only check would have silently hidden bug 4.\n\nTwo things worth flagging about confidence:\n\n- **Each fix was mutation-checked.** Reverting bug 1, 3, or 4 individually makes the matching test fail — so the regression tests aren't vacuous.\n- **~60,000 extra cases** were compared during investigation via an out-of-tree Python harness (exact tie corpora from `Fraction`/`Decimal`/`float.hex`, grammar-directed forms, random bytes, every leading byte `0x00`–`0xff`, inputs up to 2 MB). Zero mismatches remain. That scaffolding was scratch-only and has been deleted; the committed suite reproduces the coverage with deterministic in-Rust fuzzers.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified — source timestamps are unchanged, and only the prescribed `build/` directory was added. `ERRORS.md` records each mismatch with the C-vs-Rust outputs and root cause."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2271,"cache_read_input_tokens":102510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2271,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c572f2e5-566f-4f11-b6ae-10b8a15dbad5","uuid":"30bc0006-a145-4eb3-99e9-8b1f90dc5683","timestamp":"2026-08-28T16:13:26.270Z"}
